{"id":"cd93a699-5794-4de3-9c99-181270cdce3c","object":"chat.completion","created":1784998294,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    word_token_indices = case['word_token_indices']\\n    word_scores = case['word_scores']\\n    forced_words = case['forced_words']\\n    token_budget = case['token_budget']\\n    forced_tokens = sum(len(word_token_indices[i]) for i in forced_words)\\n    if forced_tokens > token_budget:\\n        return {'retained_token_indices': [], 'selected_words': [], 'tokens_used': 0}\\n    selected = set(forced_words)\\n    current_tokens = forced_tokens\\n    candidates = [i for i in range(len(word_token_indices)) if i not in selected]\\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda i: (-word_scores[i], i))\\n    for i in candidates:\\n        token_len = len(word_token_indices[i])\\n        if current_tokens + token_len <= token_budget:\\n            selected.add(i)\\n            current_tokens += token_len\\n    sorted_selected = sorted(selected)\\n    retained_tokens = []\\n    for i in sorted_selected:\\n        retained_tokens.extend(word_token_indices[i])\\n    return {\\n        'retained_token_indices': retained_tokens,\\n        'selected_words': sorted_selected,\\n        'tokens_used': current_tokens\\n    }\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task NLP-LL2-02. The input case payload has fields: word_token_indices (list of lists of integers), word_scores (list of floats), forced_words (list of integers, indices into word list), token_budget (integer). Also _case_nonce but we ignore.\n\nGoal: Select a subset of words (by index) such that:\n- forced_words must be included. If total tokens of forced words exceed token_budget, return? The problem says \"reject an infeasible forced set\". So if forced_words tokens > token_budget, we should return an error? The output schema doesn't have error field. Perhaps we need to handle gracefully? The instruction: \"reject an infeasible forced set\" might mean return empty or something? But the output contract has required fields. So we must return something. Maybe we return selected_words empty, retained_token_indices empty, tokens_used 0? Or we can return forced_words? The spec says \"Select forced words first and reject an infeasible forced set.\" I think it means if forced words exceed budget, then the selection is impossible; we should return no selection? But the output requires arrays. Probably we should return empty arrays for selected_words and retained_token_indices, and tokens_used=0. Or maybe we should include forced words even if they exceed budget? The phrase \"reject an infeasible forced set\" suggests we cannot proceed; we must not include any words. So we return empty.\n\nBut let's read carefully: \"Select forced words first and reject an infeasible forced set.\" That means if the forced set is infeasible (token count > budget), then we reject the entire selection? Possibly we return an empty result. But the output schema requires selected_words and retained_token_indices arrays. So empty arrays are fine.\n\nNow, after forced words, we have remaining words. Each word has a score (word_scores[i]) and a token group (word_token_indices[i]). We rank remaining words by descending score, then by original position (i.e., index) for ties. Then greedy: starting from residual budget (budget - forced tokens), we add words if their token group fits. We stop when no more fit. We must return:\n- retained_token_indices: list of all token indices that are selected (from all selected words, including forced), in original token index order? Actually the spec: \"Return words and tokens in original order with exact token usage.\" For retained_token_indices: should be list of integers (token indices) in original token order? The schema says items are integers, but not specifying order. Likely the token indices should be sorted ascending (original order) because that's natural. But careful: word_token_indices contains token indices that are already in order per word? The words are in order of appearance. So if we select words in original order (ascending word index), and within each word the tokens are in order, then concatenating them gives token indices in order. However, we might have selected non-consecutive words. The retained_token_indices should list all token indices in the order they appear in the original sequence. So we need to iterate over words in original index order, for each word if selected, append its token indices to list. That preserves order.\n\nSimilarly, selected_words: list of word indices (integer) in original order (ascending). Since we process forced words and then add remaining in score order but then we need to return them in original order. The spec: \"Return words and tokens in original order with exact token usage.\" So selected_words should be sorted by original index. So after we select forced words (which are at given indices) and then possibly add more words, we need to sort the selected word indices before returning. But careful: forced words are given as indices; we must include them regardless of score. The \"rank remaining words by descending supplied score and then source position\" means we consider them in that order for acceptance, but after selection we reorder by original position.\n\nAlso note: \"Treat a word group as indivisible when accounting token cost.\" So we cannot partially include a word.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Input validation? The instruction says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" But payload may have missing fields? We assume it has all. However, we should check if word_scores, word_token_indices, forced_words, token_budget exist. If not, raise? But we can't raise because function must return JSON. We could return empty. Better to simply proceed assuming they exist.\n\nWe'll parse case payload.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Check if forced_words tokens total <= token_budget. If not, return {\"retained_token_indices\": [], \"selected_words\": [], \"tokens_used\": 0}\n2. Compute forced_tokens_set = set(forced_words)\n3. selected_word_set = set(forced_words)\n4. current_token_count = sum(len(word_token_indices[i]) for i in forced_words)\n5. Build list of candidate words: indices not in forced_words. For each such index i, compute score word_scores[i], tokens = word_token_indices[i].\n6. Sort candidates by (-score, i) (desc score, asc index).\n7. For each i in sorted candidates:\n   if current_token_count + len(word_token_indices[i]) <= token_budget:\n       selected_word_set.add(i)\n       current_token_count += len(word_token_indices[i])\n8. Now produce output:\n   - Build selected_words_list = sorted(selected_word_set)\n   - Build retained_token_indices = []\n     for i in sorted(selected_word_set):\n         retained_token_indices.extend(word_token_indices[i])\n   - tokens_used = len(retained_token_indices) (should equal current_token_count)\n9. Return dict with keys \"retained_token_indices\", \"selected_words\", \"tokens_used\".\n\nNote: For forced words, if they are given in arbitrary order? The spec says \"forced_words\" is a list of indices. We treat them as a set. The order in forced_words doesn't matter for selection; we include them all.\n\nEdge cases: budget zero? Then forced_words must be empty, otherwise infeasible. If forced_words empty, we select no words.\n\nPotential nuance: \"Rank remaining words by descending supplied score and then source position.\" Source position likely means original index. We sort by (score descending, index ascending). When scores are equal, we use index. For ties in score, we want to prefer earlier index? That is typical: source position as tiebreaker. So we sort key=lambda i: (-word_scores[i], i).\n\nNow, we must produce Python source code as a JSON string. The answer must be a single JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a string containing the Python code. We need to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nWrite the code. Use only standard library. No imports needed.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The output must match schema exactly: keys in order? Not required. But must have no extra keys. The object must have exactly those three keys.\n\nWe'll write solve(case) function. Ensure it returns a dict.\n\nTesting with given fixtures (though we don't have expected outputs). We'll implement and then produce JSON.\n\nLet's write code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # Validate required fields? We'll assume they exist.\n    word_token_indices = case['word_token_indices']\n    word_scores = case['word_scores']\n    forced_words = case['forced_words']\n    token_budget = case['token_budget']\n    \n    # Compute token count for forced words\n    forced_tokens = sum(len(word_token_indices[i]) for i in forced_words)\n    if forced_tokens > token_budget:\n        return {\"retained_token_indices\": [], \"selected_words\": [], \"tokens_used\": 0}\n    \n    selected = set(forced_words)\n    current_tokens = forced_tokens\n    # Build candidate indices not in forced\n    candidates = [i for i in range(len(word_token_indices)) if i not in selected]\n    # Sort by score descending, then index ascending\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda i: (-word_scores[i], i))\n    for i in candidates:\n        token_len = len(word_token_indices[i])\n        if current_tokens + token_len <= token_budget:\n            selected.add(i)\n            current_tokens += token_len\n    \n    # Build output in original order\n    sorted_selected = sorted(selected)\n    retained_tokens = []\n    for i in sorted_selected:\n        retained_tokens.extend(word_token_indices[i])\n    return {\n        \"retained_token_indices\": retained_tokens,\n        \"selected_words\": sorted_selected,\n        \"tokens_used\": current_tokens\n    }\n\nThat's it. But careful: forced_words might contain duplicates? Not specified. We treat as set. Also note: forced_words indices m