lected:\\n        retained_tokens.extend(word_token_indices[i])\\n    return {\\n        \\\"retained_token_indices\\\": retained_tokens,\\n        \\\"selected_words\\\": sorted_selected,\\n        \\\"tokens_used\\\": current_tokens\\n    }\"\n\nBut I used double quotes inside the code. Need to escape them with backslash. In JSON, we escape double quotes as \\\" inside the string. Also the newlines as \\n. So I'll rewrite with escaped quotes.\n\nBetter to write the code using single quotes for dict keys to avoid escaping double quotes? But the code must be valid Python. Using single quotes is fine. Let's use single quotes for all dict keys in the code to avoid escaping. That simplifies.\n\nSo code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    word_token_indices = case['word_token_indices']\n    word_scores = case['word_scores']\n    forced_words = case['forced_words']\n    token_budget = case['token_budget']\n    forced_tokens = sum(len(word_token_indices[i]) for i in forced_words)\n    if forced_tokens > token_budget:\n        return {'retained_token_indices': [], 'selected_words': [], 'tokens_used': 0}\n    selected = set(forced_words)\n    current_tokens = forced_tokens\n    candidates = [i for i in range(len(word_token_indices)) if i not in selected]\n    candidates.sort(key=lambda i: (-word_scores[i], i))\n    for i in candidates:\n        token_len = len(word_token_indices[i])\n        if current_tokens + token_len <= token_budget:\n            selected.add(i)\n            current_tokens += token_len\n    sorted_selected = sorted(selected)\n    retained_tokens = []\n    for i in sorted_selected:\n        retained_tokens.extend(word_token_indices[i])\n    return {\n        'retained_token_indices': retained_tokens,\n        'selected_words': sorted_selected,\n        'tokens_used': current_tokens\n    }\n\nNow the JSON string for implementation must represent this code with escaped newlines and quotes. Since we use single quotes in code, no need to escape double quotes. The JSON